A legal assistance program can be designed as a system consisting of:
+ a set of templates with standard content, which a user fills in and generates the necessary documents in compliance with a business logic of the enterprise;
+ support for document workflow - document workflow automates internal business process. When filling in documents and generating them into other business systems, the workflow engine can be used, thus the process of document's review, approval or acceptance can be automated and tracked;
+ integrated business solutions for electronic document management;
+ rules-based automatic content analysis and extraction (key phrases, email addresses, logos);
+ support for the process of creating and publishing new and amending the documents. This process can include not only creation, but also amendment, removal and replacement, storage and security. It also involves management for document's archiving, back-up and recovery;
+ automatic notification of all participants of document's distribution.
The goal of legal reasoning is to predict the possible outcomes by employing a computer program to go over documents, such as contracts and clauses, or even a whole text and provide answers without involving a lawyer or having any background of law and knowledge. To achieve the mentioned result, computer systems need to be provided with appropriate data regarding such systems and a legal reasoning assistant can be employed.
